 :adr/validation {:measured "query-plane value-cid probe, 4 values, one negative control"
                  :route "nbb, no JVM"
                  :base "io-ipld 7caa26c / org-ietf-cbor 6cb47bd / io-multiformats ff67d85 / org-nist-sha2 becdf25 / kotobase 7e12b35"
                  :read-not-measured ["execution-contract key sets" "kotoba-delta implementation status"]}
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner brought the next iteration of the Unison x Lisp x IPLD proposal. ADR-2609081400 (2026-09-08) measured its CODE half against what already existed and found most of it landed; ADR-2609106000 (today) closed two of the rows that ADR left open. This iteration extends the same principle past code: datom, transaction, query, plan, schema, policy, patch, receipt, name, doc and test all become one kind of content-addressed object -- a `Form` -- with pure S-expression as its single human/agent notation, under six principles:\n\n1. Everything is a Form. 2. Every Form has a CID. 3. Links are CIDs. 4. Schema gives Forms meaning. 5. Evaluation produces Forms from Forms. 6. Names are data, never identity.\n\nThe code half is settled and is not re-argued here. What is new is the CROSS-PLANE claim, and one measurement decides most of it.\n\n## Where each principle already stands\n\nRead at the shas in Reproduce; principle 6 was measured today.\n\n| # | principle | state |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | everything is a Form | PARTLY, and bounded on purpose. `kotoba.value.v1` is the one value model, and its own docstring states the bound: *the codec covers the VALUE position only -- embedding structures (a semantic definition IR node, a prolly-tree leaf, a datom) keep their own envelopes and call this per value* |\n| 2 | every Form has a CID | LANDED at two granularities -- the definition (DefCID payload v2, Unison-shaped, measured in ADR-2609077000) and the value (`kotoba.value.codec/value-cid`). NOT at subexpression granularity, refused with a reason in ADR-2609081400 |\n| 3 | links are CIDs | LANDED. `ipld/link` is the tag-42 link, and kotobase already documents every block's boundary as an incidence relation whose endpoints are those links (README *Data model -- an incidence merkle graph*, ADR-2608201500) |\n| 4 | schema gives meaning | DECIDED, and since today on disk for KIR: `osaho/resources/kotoba/kir/definition-identity.ipldsch`, with the two things IPLD Schema cannot pin named in the file and the shape verdict refusing to answer checkedness (ADR-2609106000) |\n| 5 | evaluation produces Forms | LANDED, and stricter than the proposal asks -- decision 2 |\n| 6 | names are data, never identity | LANDED on the code plane and measured. **FALSE on the query plane** -- decision 3, which is the one new fact this ADR contributes |\n\n## Decision 1 -- the unification lands at the value model, not at one envelope\n\n**A single `Form` envelope shared across planes is refused. What is shared, and what every plane must call, is `kotoba.value.codec`.**\n\nThis workspace has now reached the same shape three times from three directions, and it is the same answer each time: share the layer BELOW, not the layer above.\n\n- ADR-2608039970: what the query surfaces share is the datom plane, not Datalog. Giving every surface one IR was rejected; giving them all one physical model below was not.\n- ADR-2609076000 decision 5: the DefCID seals the LOGICAL form only. Sealing the representation would move every identity on a representational change; letting the encoder vary without sealing it would give one meaning two identities.\n- `ipld.value`: one value codec, and embedding envelopes stay their own.\n\nA shared envelope is the second of those failure modes at the plane level: a datom, a query, a policy and a receipt do not mean the same kind of thing, and forcing one envelope over them either erases what distinguishes them or smuggles the distinguishing field back in as a tag -- which is an envelope again, with less checking. The proposal's real content survives this intact, because the property it wants (notation is not identity; equal values have equal addresses) is a property of the VALUE model, and that is already one thing.\n\nThe rule that follows is small and enforceable by review: **a new plane brings an envelope and calls `kotoba.value.codec` for the values inside it.** An envelope that hand-rolls its own scalar encoding is exactly the finding ADR-2609076000 exists to close, one plane over.\n\n## Decision 2 -- the universal Eval relation already exists, is versioned, and forbids precisely what the proposal would put in it\n\nThe proposal's `(eval (input Q) (evaluator E) (environment D) (output R))` is `kotobase.execution-contract` version 1, three records with exact key sets:\n\n    ExecutionManifest  #{:execution/version :data/commit :authority/policy :authority/epoch\n                         :location/manifest :schema/root :parent :issued-at :signature}\n    RequestEnvelope    #{:request/version :principal :tenant :graph :operation :query/digest\n                         :base/commit :authority/policy :authority/epoch :nonce :expires-at}\n    ExecutionReceipt   #{:receipt/version :request/digest :execution/manifest :query/plan-digest\n                         :authority/decision :result/root :cost :implementation/build :signature}\n\nThree properties of it are worth naming because the proposal reinvents two of them and would undo the third.\n\n**It separates semantic identity from physical plan.** `:query/digest` in the envelope and `:query/plan-digest` in the receipt are different fields, which is the proposal's *never give a query and its plan the same CID*, already contractual.\n\n**It refuses to carry syntax.** `forbidden-keys` rejects `:query/text`, `:query/ast`, `:query/protocol`, `:protocol`, `:backend`, `:provider`, `:credential`, `:token`, `:authorization`. The record names addresses; the AST is what an adapter translates FROM, and `execution-contract`'s docstring says adapters may translate their own ASTs into these records but may not add protocol-specific fields to the signed contract. A `Form` that carried the query's own S-expression into the receipt would put back the field the contract exists to keep out.\n\n**Its identifiers are computed, not accepted.** `bind-digest!` recomputes the envelope's digest and rejects a mismatch, on the stated ground that an envelope is evidence about a query nobody ran unless the digest is one anybody can re-derive; `kotobase.execution-identity` names the one codec (`kotoba.value.codec/value-cid`) once. And `:location/manifest` sits beside `:schema/root` rather than inside identity -- the same identity/naming/location separation ADR-2609092600 draws for the app plane, at a different layer.\n\n**So: do not add an `(eval ...)` Form. Bring a new plane onto these records.** A second answer to *what is one evaluation* is the shape decision 1 refuses, and this one is already signed, versioned and fail-closed.\n\n## Decision 3 -- names are outside identity on the code plane and INSIDE it on the query plane (measured 2026-09-10)\n\nPrinciple 6 is true where it was built and false where it was assumed. Four `kotoba.value.codec/value-cid` addresses of the same kotobase-dialect query, one per row:\n\n| probe | value | address |\n|---|---|---|\n| A one line | `[:find ?e :where [?e \"score\" ?s] [(>= ?s 25)]]` | `bafyreifdssv5uyammwfzdcgfse335hfmy7dwwnippfn3bveakfd2v4aif4` |\n| A re-indented over five lines | same value, different notation | **identical** |\n| B negative control, 25 -> 30 | | `bafyreid7zlhedza3tfqx7cr2fu2qmhjmsmgiczisjrohexkh2evxkvqzke` |\n| C `?e ?s` renamed to `?entity ?score` | same query, renamed logic variables | `bafyreihcgldobe6am4pvakh2ivv5n62bwnhqgl3h6pwte5lls63ztf46d4` |\n\nA and its re-indentation agree, so the proposal's *notation is not identity* already holds for queries with no new machinery -- an EDN query is a value, and whitespace was never in it. B moves, so the agreement in A is discriminating rather than degenerate. **C moves too.** On the code plane an equivalent edit -- rename the binder, its parameter and its call site -- leaves the DefCID byte-identical (ADR-2609077000 property 2), because binders are renamed to de Bruijn positions before sealing. The query plane has no such pass: `?e` and `?entity` are two symbols, so they are two values, so they are two addresses.\n\nThat is not a latent detail. `:query/digest` IS this address, computed by `bind-digest!`. **Two alpha-equivalent queries therefore mint two RequestEnvelopes, two ExecutionReceipts, and a miss in anything keyed on the digest.**\n\n**This ADR does not call it a defect and does not fix it.** Both readings are defensible: the digest names the query as it was submitted, which is what an auditor re-derives, and per-request records were never meant to be shared between requesters. What is not defensible is leaving the asymmetry unwritten, because principle 6 reads as a property of the system and is a property of one plane.\n\nThe cost of changing it is known in shape, from the neighbouring case: normalizing query identity moves every digest that has ever been stored or signed, the same way adopting `kotoba.value.v1` in the DefCID payload would move every frozen vector and module lock (ADR-2609076000 decision 6). That is an owner decision with a version bump attached, not a tidy-up.\n\nAnd it has a precondition that is load-bearing in its own right. To normalize `?e` away, something must know which symbols are BINDING positions -- which means reading the clause structure rather than the value. Today the clause is a positional triple; ADR-2608201500 records that role, sign and mult are erased at exactly that step and that **erasing labels is not reversible**. So query-identity normalization and the labelled-boundary generalization are the same piece of work approached from two sides, and the order in which they are done is not a matter of taste.\n\n## Decision 4 -- per-node identity stays refused, and the reason extends to the data planes\n\nADR-2609081400 refused a CID for every parenthesised node, on the ground that a subterm under a binder is an OPEN term and content-addressing it naively is unsound. That argument is about binders, so it does not transfer verbatim to a datom, which is closed and already has an address as a block. The refusal still holds for a different reason: giving every `()` an address ACROSS planes creates a second identity space over the same meanings, which is decision 5 of ADR-2609076000 restated. The proposal's actual use -- an agent that edits by naming a node instead of regenerating a file -- needs a PATH into an already-sealed block, and `ipld.selector` / `ipld.graph` already produce bounded traversals with CAR/GraphSync proof blocks.\n\n## Decision 5 -- the name `Form` is not available in this workspace\n\nTwo live meanings already hold it. `kotoba-lang/forms` is an office product with a Pages editor and a `:forms/form` resource kind in the shared Kotoba office envelope. And *forms* is the existing name for reader output in the compiler pipeline that `kotoba-sema` documents as `source bytes -> forms -> semantic analysis -> checked kotoba-hir`. Naming the cross-plane object `Form` would make `forms.wire/form-envelope` and *the form plane* the same word for unrelated things, in a workspace whose naming rule already says a name that does not indicate its function must announce itself in its README and register in `manifest/concept-vocabulary.edn`.\n\nThe planes already have names -- value, definition, datom, commit, query, plan, manifest, receipt, grant -- and decision 1 means there is no new object that needs one.\n\n## What the proposal is right about and is not built\n\n**Schema is addressed but not queryable.** `:schema/root` is a field of the signed ExecutionManifest, so a schema already HAS an address in the record that governs an execution. What does not exist is a query surface that answers *does Person have an email field* from that address. The proposal's schema-as-datoms is that missing half, and it is buildable without inventing an identity, because the identity is already in the manifest.\n\n**The labelled boundary.** The proposal's closing move -- read `(f a b)` as an incidence relation with labelled endpoints rather than a head-plus-arguments syntax -- is the accepted framing in kotobase's data model already, and ADR-2608201500 records exactly where it stops: the query layer's clauses are positional triples, so role, sign and mult have nowhere to sit. That is the one item on this list where waiting has a cost, per decision 3.\n\n**Patch and derivation as data.** `kotoba-delta` (ADR-2607161325) records a signed discrete op-log, deliberately not a text CRDT. Its ADR states *accepted, implemented*; that status was READ here, not measured.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n\n- The probe measured the kotobase dialect only. The SQL, SPARQL, Cypher and GraphQL surfaces were not measured, and one of them may normalize its variables.\n- No claim that normalizing query identity is desirable. The claim is that the asymmetry exists, has a measurable consequence at `:query/digest`, and is currently unwritten.\n- The key sets and `forbidden-keys` were READ from source at the sha below. No live governed execution was run.\n- `kotoba-delta`'s implementation status was read from its ADR, not measured.\n- Nothing here is a gate. No check asserts that a new plane's envelope goes through `kotoba.value.codec`, and this ADR does not invent one.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nDecision 3, at `io-ipld` `7caa26c`, `org-ietf-cbor` `6cb47bd`, `io-multiformats` `ff67d85`, `org-nist-sha2` `becdf25`, `text` `73bdb13`-pinned checkout -- each 0 behind its last-fetched default branch, each clean:\n\n    nbb --classpath \"orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipld/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-cbor/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/io-multiformats/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-nist-sha2/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/text/src\" <probe>\n\nwhere the probe prints `(vc/value-cid q)` for the four values in the table above. Keep the re-indented pair AND the 25 -> 30 control: a comparison that only ever reports agreement cannot be told from one that never ran. `kotoba.lang.text` must be on the classpath or `io-ipld` will not load, which is a missing dependency rather than a codec answer.\n\nDecisions 2 and 5, at `kotobase` `7e12b35` and `ayatori` `91bcd02`:\n\n    sed -n '1,45p' orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase/src/kotobase/execution_contract.cljc\n    sed -n '170,190p' orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase/src/kotobase/governed_execution.cljc\n    sed -n '1,15p'  orgs/kotoba-lang/forms/README.md\n"}]
